ple mountain path, but a part of the garden path. If Jingma continued to walk on the gravel path, he would end up behind Ouying. He didn't want to be suspected of approaching Ouying, so he decided to take the side path for a walk. [1544] The side path was different from the flat garden path, it was a winding uphill path. Jingma's surroundings were no longer the trimmed garden trees, but the dense forest. He walked very hard because his shoes were uncomfortable. [1545] It's not just a simple mountain path, Jingma became uneasy. At that moment, a pair of stone lanterns appeared on both sides of the road. The top of the hat stone had a dragon decoration, which was a strange lantern. It looked quite old, with the corners worn by wind and rain, a bit round, covered with moss, and the fire bag was empty, which indicated that it could no longer be used as a lantern. [1546] Further ahead, beyond the lanterns, was an upward slope, like a mountain temple's approach, with wooden planks laid across it to form steps. At the end of the path was an ancient shrine. [1547] It seemed to be the ancient shrine mentioned by Rock Cave. It was about the same size as a small shrine, not quite two rooms wide. It had a high bed style, a simple and rustic bird-shaped design, and a wooden staircase on the front door. Unlike the shrine, it was used for both living and sleeping, and was more like a common small shrine. [1548] The entrance was a double-door lattice gate, with a big lock hanging on it. The door was old, but the lock looked new. From the narrow gaps between the lattice, it was too dark to see inside. [1549] Since the road ended here, this detour must have been the approach to the ancient shrine. So, the source of the origin of the nest must be nearby. Although the quiet horse was interested in it, he didn't want to explore this place that was considered the sacred land of the nest, as he didn't know what kind of criticism he would face afterwards. [1550] Reluctantly, the quiet horse turned around, and in the distant distance, he saw a silhouette of a climber emerging from between the trees. The quiet horse hurriedly hid behind a tree nearby, realizing that if he was discovered in this state, he would be even more suspicious. However, at this point, the footsteps approached the lanterns, and even if the quiet horse went out immediately, it was too late. [1551] The calm horse prayed silently to himself that he would not be discovered by the approaching person, while peering out from the shade of the tree. It was Mizuho who arrived. She looked around curiously at the surroundings in front of the stone lantern, then crouched down behind one of the stone lanterns on the side. It was unclear what she was doing, but after about ten seconds, she stood up and ran back the way she came. [1552] This even stranger action made Jingma pause. After a while, he went down to investigate the lantern where Cui Sui had squatted, but found nothing particularly strange. [1553] What's going on...? Jinma came back and told Yuyin about this. [1554] \"You also have times when you can be helpful.\" [1555] Yuying spat out a surprising line from her mouth and then lavishly praised Jingma. The praise was so exaggerated that it almost seemed like a joke. [1556] \"Then, let's go and see now.\" [1557] \"As soon as the words were spoken, Yuying jumped up and tucked the fan into his chest. Jingma's heels were rubbed raw from the shoes, and he didn't want to climb the mountain path again, but as an apprentice assistant, he had no right to refuse.\" [1558] \"There's no need to change your shoes specially.\" [1559] Jingma was just about to walk to the main house when Ouying's voice came from behind him. She said that if you go around the fence outside the courtyard, you can enter the mountain without changing your shoes. After all, only the garden cannot be entered with shoes worn in daily life. [1560] \"Well, that's just a scratch!\" [1561] Shin'ichi couldn't help but complain. [1562] \"You were in the garden just now, weren't you?\" said Oyuki, ignoring Shin'ichi's mood. [1563] There was nothing more frustrating than hearing a perfectly reasonable explanation at a time like this. Nevertheless, Oyuki continued on ahead, walking towards the fence. [1564] \"I know the way, I was there just yesterday.\" [1565] Oyuki's clear voice carried over the wind to Shin'ichi's ears. [1566]\"What? You've already been there? Have you been taking a stroll too, Yuying?\" [1567]\"What are you talking about? As a detective, it's natural for me to check out the house.\" [1568]\"So it is. I thought I had a new discovery\u2026\"\n[1569]Jinma was disappointed. [1570]\"Only you, Jinma, wouldn't know it? The police don't need to say, even my father has taken a walk and knows where it is.\" [1571] \"Oh\u2026\"\n[1572] Jingma became even more depressed. [1573] The first time it took about twenty to thirty minutes, but this time it only took fifteen minutes to reach the stone lantern, probably because the path was remembered. [1574] Oyama pointed to the stone lantern on the right and asked, \"Is it true that Mrs. Menuz was squatting behind this lantern?\" [1575] Jingma nodded. Oyama squatted behind the stone lantern in the same way, placing his hands on his knees and observing for a moment without blinking. [1576] \"There's something off about the color scheme. The gradient is reversed.\" [1577] Yuying muttered, and stuck his hand into the gap between the base and the pedestal. As a result, a part of the pedestal fell off, revealing a fifteen-centimeter square hole in the depths of the lantern. [1578] \"Is it a secret door?\" [1579] \"It's not so remarkable, it just looks worn out and broken just right. Although, considering how little dirt it has accumulated, it must have been used as a storage place. It's empty now.\" [1580] \"So, you mean Lady Meizhu hid something here?\" [1581] \"If anything was hidden here, I can think of it being something she temporarily hid here. Maybe it's not related to the case. Although, if she's suspected because of it, that would be a problem. Or maybe someone else hid something here that Mrs. Ueno stole or received. If it was stolen, then she and that person are opposed; if it was received, then they are in cahoots.\" [1582] \"So, you're saying that Mrs. Ueno is somehow involved in the case, right? She said she wouldn't hide anything and would be fair. Let's question her about it.\" [1583] However, Yuki stood up, showing no intention of questioning Mrs. Ueno. [1584] \"I'll ask her eventually, but not now. It's wiser to wait and see. And who knows, maybe something else will be hidden here.\" [1585] It seems that Yuki's strategy is to pretend to know nothing. Moreover, for some reason, she gives the impression that she wants to protect Mrs. Ueno. [1586] \"And, for her sake, I'll say this: fairness, in the end, is just a line drawn by each person's subjective judgment. Even if our understanding of fairness is different, it's nothing to be surprised about.\" [1587] \"Is this a kind of mutual understanding? But... maybe we can find out something if we lay in wait here.\" [1588] \"It's worth a try to lay in wait. However, if Mrs. Uzuki only hid something temporarily here, you'll be left with nothing. And even if she came here to receive something, we don't know when they'll use it again. The only hope is that Mrs. Uzuki stole something from someone else just now, and that person hasn't realized it yet. Then, that person will come here to think about the things they hid here in the near future.\" [1589] \"It's really a slim hope.\" [1590] Shigemaru sighed. [1591] \"But detectives don't mind doing these futile things. Because they extract the truth from the accumulation of useless things, that's their method.\" [1592] \"You're praising them quite a bit.\" [1593] This seemed to touch a nerve with Oyama. She glared at Shizuma with a serious look she hadn't seen before:\n[1594] \"Don't forget, my father was 