 pieces. [7] The villagers were surprised and asked the daughter why she could accomplish what her mother could not. The mother answered that although she inherited a large amount of power from the gods, it was not enough to eliminate another god. It was only because Qiyao mixed excellent human blood that she could eliminate the dragon. Although Qiyao only inherited half of her mother's power, she inherited her father's entire power. To eliminate the dragon, the power of the gods and human power were both necessary. [8] At the age of forty, Qiyao passed her title to her daughter and descended to become an ordinary person. The daughter also inherited half of her mother's power and all of human power. Although Qiyao had a son, he could only inherit half of his mother's power and not his father's, so he could not succeed Qiyao's position. Moreover, Qiyao said that if the person who could best inherit her power inherited her legacy, the village would be long-lasting. [9] Since then, the Qiyao family has had female descendants inherit their power and have properly governed the village. This is the current Qiyao family. [10] * * *\n[11][1] God era: In Japanese mythology, it refers to the era before Emperor Jimmu ascended the throne, i.e., the mythical era. [12] 1\n[13] Sh\u014dta Hosono visited the hot spring of Penglai for the second time. The first time was two years ago. He had an old-fashioned hobby of traveling to hot springs, so he and his university friend got along well. During the winter break of two years ago, they spent an entire month wandering around a cold, obscure hot spring resort in the Shinsh\u016b region. Their trip was quite modest, and they earned their travel expenses by working at various destinations. They carefully investigated travel guide magazines and chose cheap yet seemingly pleasant inns to stay at. Although they occasionally hitchhiked when they were short on money, food was the only exception, and they insisted on having dishes with a certain level of quality. [14] They had endured many hardships such as getting rained on and walking for two hours, but it was precisely because of this that their satisfaction was particularly strong and had become a beautiful memory of their lives. After walking nearly twenty hot spring resorts, the hot spring they visited on the eve of the end of their trip was Penglai. [15] Penglai was a small hot spring inn, so it was not listed in any travel guides. Initially, they had no intention of visiting it, but their plan changed suddenly when the innkeeper of the inn they stayed at the previous night mentioned a secret hot spring that was unknown to the public. Penglai was not only the hot spring inn but also the village of Hikari, which was unknown. The rough map of the travel guide did not even write the village name, but only drew two lines in front of the triangular mark indicating the mountain top, one of which was a river and the other was a road that abruptly ended. [16] \"This place is a hidden gem,\" said the friend, grinning. [17] However, when they arrived in Yamauchi Village after several bus transfers, they were surprised to find that it was even smaller than they had imagined. The village, nestled in a low-lying valley surrounded by steep mountains, was sparsely populated with scattered houses. The river flowing through the village divided it in two, with only a small patch of flat land on which rice paddies and terraced fields were laid out. The two hills on either side approached from above, their snow-capped peaks obscuring the sky. They got off the bus and asked a local for directions. The villager looked at them curiously and pointed upstream along the river. When they asked for an explanation, the villager said that although a few tourists came to the hot spring for healing, they all drove there. It was only natural, as the road to the inn was not accessible by public transportation, and Kenji Maeda and his friend had to hike for about thirty minutes with their backpacks. [18] That's all the impression Kenji Maeda has of Kotoyu Onsen from two years ago. He remembers the route well, but he can't recall the exact structure of the inn or the outdoor hot spring. Everything was old and well-maintained, but there were no other guests, so the atmosphere was excellent \u2013 that's all that remains in his foggy memory. Despite the hot spring and inn being good, they didn't leave a lasting impression. Since it's a small village, there were no opportunities to work, so they left hastily after staying for just one night. This is why the memory is vague. The run-down hot spring inn was far from being a gold mine. [19] Therefore, just a month ago, Kotoyu Onsen was almost forgotten by Kenji Maeda. He suddenly remembered Kotoyu Onsen because of the old story the innkeeper told him. [20] \"Where are you going today, Mr. Kenji Maeda?\" [21] Jumon was about to leave the inn when the innkeeper, Jidai Kouzawa, politely approached him. He was a rough-looking mountain man of about thirty-five years old, but his tone and attitude were quite gentle. [22] His feet were wrapped around a pet called Tsuki. Tsuki was a pure white snowy weasel about twenty centimeters long, with only the tip of its tail black. Last year, it was found injured in a stone mortar in the town of Yachiyo and was named Tsuki. It was said that snowy weasels were wary animals, but perhaps it was because Jidai took care of it when it was still young, so it was very docile and friendly. It is now kept as a mascot of Otofu. [23] \"I'm going to see the Dragon's Head again.\" [24] \"The Dragon's Head, huh\u2026\"\n[25] Kouzawa showed a \"again?\" expression. It was also understandable, as Jumon had been going to the Dragon's Head for the past four days. [26] Quiet Horse had been staying here for three days. It was not a place where a young man of twenty would typically spend much time alone. To avoid suspicion, Quiet Horse had used the excuse that he was conducting field research for his graduation thesis, but it was unclear whether Kichijiro had any suspicions. Although Kichijiro mentioned that scholars occasionally visited the place, it was possible that he frequently approached Quiet Horse to monitor his activities. [27] \"But isn't there anything that needs to be investigated so much in such a secluded place?\" [28] \"Hmm, it's for field research, kind of like a graduation practice.\" [29] Quiet Horse averted his gaze and casually brushed off the question. [30] \"Well, if you need to observe something, why not borrow my fishing gear? You can fish while observing, and there are often salmon in that area.\" [31] Kurihara made a motion with his muscular arm, imitating casting a fishing rod. He is tall and muscular, with arms and legs twice as thick as Jingma's, and has a sturdy, dark complexion with a gentle, downcast expression. His appearance and cheerful character together create the genuine, wholesome aura of a healthy, rustic country man. During hunting season, he enters the mountains with a single-handed rifle. This time is no exception. Kurihara's boar and deer kills were turned into hotpot for dinner. He mentioned that he is married but has no children, and that his parents passed away. When Jingma first came, Kurihara and his wife, Akiko, took care of the household chores together, but Akiko fell ill a year ago and has been bedridden ever since, leaving Kurihara to manage things on his own. [32] When Jingma heard this on his first day here, he expressed sympathy and said, \"That's tough.\" Kurihara, however, laughed heartily and replied, \"Actually, it's fortunate that the store is quiet, so it's not very strenuous. When a few customers come at once, the village folks come to help.\" [33] \"Fishing? I've never fished before.\" [34] \"Never?\" Kurihara, who grew up in the village, seemed incredulous and widened his eyes, \"But if you try, you'll find that fishing is surprisingly easy. I have a secret bait recipe. With artificial lures, you need various skills, but with edible bait, it's not so difficult. This summer, an elderly guest over sixty years old caught six salmon in two hours and was delighted. He said he hadn't fished since he was a child.\" [35] Feeling that refusing would prolong the conversation, Jingma borrowed the fishing gear. After all, there was nothing to do today, and fishing might be a good way to pass the time. [36] J\u016bmi took out the fishing gear from the house and gave Jir\u014d a brief lesson on how to use it. He then added, \"I'll make a dish out of the fish you catch tonight. Although it's past the fishing season, I promise it'll be delicious. You've been eating a lot of meat lately, so you must be getting tired of it. Anyway, with that bait, you'll be able to catch fish.\" [37] He added, \"You'll be able to catch fish even if you're clumsy,\" as if he was already thinking about how to prepare the fish. [38] Jir\u014d reluctantly shouldered the fishing gear with his right shoulder and headed towards the Dragon's Head in the winter sunlight. Once December arrives, the mountains turn gray, and the vibrant life force disappears. This is the season of \"endurance,\" but Jir\u014d began to wonder, \"Do I really want to survive?\" [39] He walked along the narrow shortcut by the riverbank, starting from Gion-no-yu, heading north. After about fifteen minutes, he arrived at a slightly open area. The stream curved suddenly upstream, forming a pond, and the inside became a small riverbank. This place is called Dragon's Pond. Only the faint sound of the waterfall could be heard in the pond, and there was no human presence. Only the lonely cries of birds and the rustling of the needles could be heard in the wind. [40]\n[41] The branches stretched almost to the sky, and the inside of the pond, although it was a riverbank, was a place of rough stones rolling around. It was not a leisurely place to spread out a plastic sheet and have lunch. Moreover, on the outside of the pond, where the water flowed more rapidly, there was a cliff that stood upright like a knife, blocking the view. Behind the riverbank was the steep slope of the mountain, and the road to the upstream was cut off here, so the feeling of being in a dead end was very vivid. [42] At the very back of the riverbank, facing the direction of the pond, there was a huge rock that rose from the slope of the mountain, pointing straight up to the sky. This rock, which seemed to suddenly appear from between the trees, was about four meters high, with its front end extending towards the pond at a certain angle. Due to the narrowness of the riverbank, it felt even stronger than its appearance suggested. This dark gray rock that dominated the surroundings was called the Dragon's Head. [43] Although it was a rock with a width that could not be reached even by spreading one's arms, it tapered off neatly and smoothly towards the top, with only the front end slightly curved, extending horizontally towards the pond. It was said that its shape resembled a beheaded dragon and was named the Dragon's Head. [44] In that case, it should be \"Dragon's Body\" rather than \"Dragon's Head.\" However, according to the legend of the village, the dragon that had brought disaster to the village was killed, and its head turned into this rock. The head was buried under the rock. [45] The lower part of the large rock was hollowed out, with a cavity about fifty centimeters high and thirty centimeters deep. From another angle, it could be interpreted as a shrine for the dragon's head. However, although it was a legendary relic, there were no sacred ropes or signs on the riverbank. Unless you had heard the story beforehand, people would probably walk by without paying any attention, thinking \"It's just a strange rock.\" [46] Jingma sat down on a suitable rock beside him and looked up at Dragon's Head. The sunlight that spilled through the branches blocking the sky cast dappled shadows on the rock surface,